WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.360 --> 00:00:04.919
Hello everyone. Welcome to the Langfocus channel and my name is Paul.

00:00:04.919 --> 00:00:10.349
Today's topic is the languages of South
Africa. The Republic of South Africa

00:00:10.349 --> 00:00:14.940
covers much of the southern tip of the
African continent and is a place where

00:00:14.940 --> 00:00:19.199
numerous native African cultures as well
as former colonial populations have

00:00:19.199 --> 00:00:24.689
converged, resulting in a state of true
linguistic diversity. South Africa has 11

00:00:24.689 --> 00:00:28.019
official languages that have been
recognized in its Constitution since

00:00:28.019 --> 00:00:31.859
1993. If that seems like a lot to you,
you're probably right

00:00:31.859 --> 00:00:35.670
South Africa is among the countries with
the largest number of official languages

00:00:35.670 --> 00:00:40.080
at the national level. Number one is
actually neighbouring Zimbabwe with

00:00:40.080 --> 00:00:44.760
sixteen official languages, some of which
are also official in South Africa.

00:00:44.760 --> 00:00:49.110
These are the 11 official languages of South Africa in order of most spoken to least

00:00:49.110 --> 00:00:55.950
spoken, as a first language. Zulu, Xhosa,
Afrikaans, English, Northern Sotho, Tswana,

00:00:55.950 --> 00:01:03.630
Southern Sotho, Tsonga, Swazi, Venda and
Ndebele. Over 99% of South Africans

00:01:03.630 --> 00:01:07.440
speak one of these languages as a first
language. All of these languages are

00:01:07.440 --> 00:01:11.580
members of the southern Bantu branch of
the Niger-Congo language family with the

00:01:11.580 --> 00:01:15.840
exception of Afrikaans and English which
are Germanic languages brought to the

00:01:15.840 --> 00:01:20.280
country by Europeans. The Dutch first
arrived in what is now South Africa in

00:01:20.280 --> 00:01:23.610
1652, establishing the Cape Colony in the

00:01:23.610 --> 00:01:27.870
western portion of the country. Also
living in this area were native Khoisan

00:01:27.870 --> 00:01:32.130
people and other Africans who spoke
Bantu languages. The British seized

00:01:32.130 --> 00:01:37.560
control of the Cape Colony in 1815 and
British settlers began to arrive. The

00:01:37.560 --> 00:01:41.909
Boers - the descendants of the original
Dutch settlers - disliked many aspects of

00:01:41.909 --> 00:01:45.780
British rule, but one of them was the
Anglicization of the Cape Colony and the

00:01:45.780 --> 00:01:50.100
attempt to force English on the Boers as
a medium of instruction in schools. They

00:01:50.100 --> 00:01:54.270
wanted to continue to use Dutch as their
medium of instruction and to continue

00:01:54.270 --> 00:01:58.020
speaking their distinct dialect of Dutch
called Cape Dutch which would later

00:01:58.020 --> 00:02:03.450
evolve into Afrikaans. Boers began the
Great Trek, migrating inland and creating

00:02:03.450 --> 00:02:07.409
their own Boer Republics in an area
more densely populated with African

00:02:07.409 --> 00:02:12.030
tribes who spoke Bantu languages. The
British later took control of the Boer

00:02:12.030 --> 00:02:14.730
Republics and created the Union of South Africa in

00:02:14.730 --> 00:02:19.290
1910. That eventually led to the creation
of the Republic of South Africa. From

00:02:19.290 --> 00:02:23.489
that point on there were two official
languages: English and Dutch. But Dutch

00:02:23.489 --> 00:02:26.489
as spoken by the Boers
had evolved into a distinct language

00:02:26.489 --> 00:02:32.040
called Afrikaans, which replaced Dutch as
an official language in 1925. These two

00:02:32.040 --> 00:02:36.180
colonial languages became the prestige
languages of South Africa and speakers

00:02:36.180 --> 00:02:39.450
of other languages didn't really have
any say in the matter. During the

00:02:39.450 --> 00:02:43.560
apartheid era schools were segregated
not only based on race but also based on

00:02:43.560 --> 00:02:47.370
mother tongue. Native speakers of English
and Afrikaans were taught in their

00:02:47.370 --> 00:02:49.950
native language throughout their
schooling, with second language

00:02:49.950 --> 00:02:54.269
instruction in the other official
language. Black South Africans were

00:02:54.269 --> 00:02:57.959
required to study in their mother tongue
for most of their primary school years,

00:02:57.959 --> 00:03:03.239
after which point they normally studied
in English. But in 1974 the apartheid

00:03:03.239 --> 00:03:07.049
government implemented a new policy
requiring instruction in black schools

00:03:07.049 --> 00:03:11.700
to be conducted in a 50/50 split between
English and Afrikaans, with the language

00:03:11.700 --> 00:03:15.540
of instruction depending on the course.
This was implemented without consulting

00:03:15.540 --> 00:03:19.970
the black population, and opposition to
this move led to the Soweto uprising of

00:03:19.970 --> 00:03:25.590
1976. Afrikaans was seen as the language
of the oppressor since the apartheid

00:03:25.590 --> 00:03:29.489
government was dominated by white
speakers of Afrikaans, while English was

00:03:29.489 --> 00:03:34.139
seen as a more cosmopolitan language.
When apartheid ended in the early 1990s

00:03:34.139 --> 00:03:38.069
a new constitution was written that
added nine additional African languages

00:03:38.069 --> 00:03:41.599
as official languages in order to be
more inclusive of the entire population,

00:03:41.599 --> 00:03:46.560
The education system now is based on
freedom of choice with every individual

00:03:46.560 --> 00:03:50.639
free to choose which of the 11 official
languages to be educated in for the

00:03:50.639 --> 00:03:54.180
first part of their schooling, as long as
there's sufficient demand for the school

00:03:54.180 --> 00:03:57.750
to provide it. That means that they don't
have to study in their mother tongue if

00:03:57.750 --> 00:04:02.220
they don't want to. Let's take a look at
the map of South Africa. When apartheid

00:04:02.220 --> 00:04:06.510
was abolished, the four provinces of
South Africa and the homelands - enclaves

00:04:06.510 --> 00:04:11.310
for African ethnic groups - were abolished
and nine new provinces were created. This

00:04:11.310 --> 00:04:15.629
map shows the most dominant languages in South Africa down to the local level. You

00:04:15.629 --> 00:04:19.500
can see that the 11 official languages
are clustered mainly in certain areas

00:04:19.500 --> 00:04:23.490
and may predominate in certain provinces,
but they don't correspond exactly to

00:04:23.490 --> 00:04:25.770
provincial orders you can see that Afrikaans

00:04:25.770 --> 00:04:30.300
predominates in the West while the Bantu
languages predominate in the east and

00:04:30.300 --> 00:04:35.520
English predominates in South Africa's
major urban centers. As you can see

00:04:35.520 --> 00:04:38.910
there's quite an intricate linguistic
situation in the eastern part of the

00:04:38.910 --> 00:04:42.900
country so let's zoom in and look at the
African languages there. As I mentioned

00:04:42.900 --> 00:04:46.860
earlier, the non-European languages of
South Africa are mainly members of the

00:04:46.860 --> 00:04:50.729
southern Bantu branch of the Niger-Congo
language family and these account for

00:04:50.729 --> 00:04:55.800
about 70% of the population, or around 40
million people. These southern Bantu

00:04:55.800 --> 00:04:59.820
languages display some features typical
of the Bantu languages in general. They

00:04:59.820 --> 00:05:04.889
follow an SVO word order; like almost all
Bantu languages their tonal; and they are

00:05:04.889 --> 00:05:08.729
largely agglutinative and feature a
large number of a fixes to nouns and

00:05:08.729 --> 00:05:14.009
verbs. Most Bantu languages do not have
click sounds but many of these southern

00:05:14.509 --> 00:05:18.960
Bantu languages do as a result of
contact with the Khoisan languages. The

00:05:18.960 --> 00:05:22.590
nine main southern Bantu languages of
South Africa can be further categorized

00:05:22.590 --> 00:05:27.240
into three smaller subdivisions: the Nguni languages; the Sotho-Tswana

00:05:27.240 --> 00:05:33.180
languages; and the Tswa-Ronga or Tsonga
group. Venda forms its own group, so there

00:05:33.180 --> 00:05:37.710
are actually four subdivisions including
this one. The Nguni branch of the

00:05:37.710 --> 00:05:41.820
southern Bantu tree contains four of
South Africa's official languages: Zulu,

00:05:41.820 --> 00:05:47.460
Xhosa, Swazi, and Ndebele. The first
two are the two most spoken languages

00:05:47.460 --> 00:05:52.080
within South Africa as a first language,
with Zulu representing 23% of the

00:05:52.080 --> 00:05:57.300
population and Xhosa representing 16% of
it. The other two on the other hand are

00:05:57.300 --> 00:06:03.090
spoken by only 2.5 percent in 2.1
percent, respectively. Zulu is mainly

00:06:03.090 --> 00:06:08.460
spoken in Kwazulu-Natal and most of Mpumalanga, where Swazi and Ndebele

00:06:08.460 --> 00:06:13.590
are spoken as well. Xhosa is the majority
language of the Eastern Cape but has

00:06:13.590 --> 00:06:17.070
communities of speakers in other
provinces as well, as other official

00:06:17.070 --> 00:06:22.169
languages do. The Nguni languages
feature click consonants. You can hear

00:06:22.169 --> 00:06:26.280
one of them in the name of the language
Xhosa. Actually, this is the first time

00:06:26.280 --> 00:06:29.610
I've tried to pronounce a click
consonant so I may be pronouncing the

00:06:29.610 --> 00:06:32.940
name of the language wrong from the
point of view of a native speaker. But I

00:06:32.940 --> 00:06:37.950
guess it's something like that: Xhosa. All
of the Nguni languages are mutually

00:06:37.950 --> 00:06:40.168
intelligible to a large extent.

00:06:40.168 --> 00:06:43.530
Sotho-Tswana. The second largest of the southern

00:06:43.530 --> 00:06:47.310
Bantu branches is the Sotho-Tswana
branch which has three languages within

00:06:47.310 --> 00:06:52.950
it: Northern Sotho - representing 9.1% of
South Africa's population; Tswana, with 8%;

00:06:52.950 --> 00:06:59.250
and Southern Sotho - or simply Sotho - with
7.6%. Altogether they represent just

00:06:59.250 --> 00:07:02.970
under a quarter of the population of
South Africa: around 13.6

00:07:02.970 --> 00:07:07.350
million people. Each of these three
languages serves as the majority

00:07:07.350 --> 00:07:11.340
language in the province that it's most
widely spoken in. Southern Sotho in the

00:07:11.340 --> 00:07:15.840
Free State; Tswana in the northwest
province, and there is also a significant

00:07:15.840 --> 00:07:19.680
population of speakers in the Northern
Cape with which it shares a border; and

00:07:19.680 --> 00:07:25.470
Northern Sotho in Limpopo. Southern Sotho contains click consonants due to

00:07:25.470 --> 00:07:29.490
contact with Nguni languages, while
some urban varieties of Northern Sotho

00:07:29.490 --> 00:07:33.810
have in recent times been acquiring
clicks as well. Tswana uses some click

00:07:33.810 --> 00:07:38.640
sounds, but only in interjections and
ideophones (onomatopoeia, for example). And

00:07:38.640 --> 00:07:42.840
like the Nguni languages, the Sotho-Tswana languages are generally

00:07:42.840 --> 00:07:48.240
mutually intelligible as well. Tsonga or
Tswa-Ronga. Although there are other

00:07:48.240 --> 00:07:52.260
languages within this branch, the only
one that has official status is Tsonga.

00:07:52.260 --> 00:07:56.070
Other languages of this group are
mutually intelligible and are oftentimes

00:07:56.070 --> 00:07:59.910
considered part of the Tsonga
language. Found in the Northeast,

00:07:59.910 --> 00:08:04.530
most of the Sangha speaking people live
in Limpopo or Mpumalanga. Tsonga

00:08:04.530 --> 00:08:06.570
features click sounds in just a few
words.

00:08:06.570 --> 00:08:11.040
Another interesting phonological feature
is whistled sibilant consonants. I

00:08:11.040 --> 00:08:14.970
haven't been able to find a sample from
Tsonga, but here's a sample of a similar

00:08:14.970 --> 00:08:17.990
sound in the Shona language.

00:08:17.990 --> 00:08:19.990
&lt;Whistling sibilant example&gt;

00:08:26.950 --> 00:08:31.630
Venda. One of the less spoken of the
official languages in South Africa, Venda

00:08:31.630 --> 00:08:36.940
comprises 2.4 percent of the population,
or 1.3 million speakers who are almost

00:08:36.940 --> 00:08:41.410
all concentrated on the northern border
of Limpopo, near zimbabwe where its

00:08:41.910 --> 00:08:45.900
closest linguistic relatives live. Venda
has no click sounds.

00:08:45.900 --> 00:08:47.400
Afrikaans and English

00:08:47.500 --> 00:08:51.400
The two Germanic languages of
South Africa, Afrikaans and English, are

00:08:51.400 --> 00:08:55.540
the third and fourth most spoken
languages in the country, with 13.5

00:08:55.540 --> 00:08:59.610
percent and 9.6 percent of citizens
speaking them as their first language

00:08:59.610 --> 00:09:04.300
respectively. People outside of South
Africa might assume that native speakers

00:09:04.300 --> 00:09:08.680
of Afrikaans are all white, but that's
not the case. It's the majority language

00:09:08.680 --> 00:09:12.070
in the Northern Cape and Western Cape
provinces where most of the nation's

00:09:12.070 --> 00:09:16.840
colored population, or mixed-race
population, lives and more than 75

00:09:16.840 --> 00:09:21.000
percent of coloured South Africans speak
Afrikaans as their first language.

00:09:21.000 --> 00:09:22.200
Why is that?

00:09:22.300 --> 00:09:26.410
It's because they were part of the
languages development. The origin of the

00:09:26.410 --> 00:09:29.770
coloured community lies in the early
days of the Cape Colony when Dutch

00:09:29.770 --> 00:09:33.400
settlers intermarried with indigenous
Khoisan women or slaves from Africa,

00:09:33.400 --> 00:09:38.020
India, and Southeast Asia. But they also
interacted linguistically, and by

00:09:38.020 --> 00:09:41.410
learning Dutch imperfectly as a second
language they influenced the direction

00:09:41.410 --> 00:09:46.000
of the Dutch dialect that would evolve
into Afrikaans. It's also spoken as a

00:09:46.000 --> 00:09:49.690
native language by smaller numbers of
the South Asian and black South African

00:09:49.690 --> 00:09:54.280
populations, and it's spoken by over 60%
of the white population as a native

00:09:54.280 --> 00:09:58.690
language. In total it's the first
language of about 13.5 percent of the

00:09:58.690 --> 00:10:02.770
population and it's spoken by an even
larger number as a second language. If

00:10:02.770 --> 00:10:07.150
you haven't seen my language profile on
Afrikaans from a couple of years ago, you

00:10:07.150 --> 00:10:10.900
can check it out right here (click link at the top).
English is spoken as a native language

00:10:10.900 --> 00:10:15.910
mainly in the Western Cape Gauteng and
Kwazulu-Natal, as these are where the

00:10:15.910 --> 00:10:20.220
major cities can be found. Plus it's
widely spoken as a second language.

00:10:20.220 --> 00:10:24.760
English is the native language of 9.6
percent of the population, including

00:10:24.760 --> 00:10:29.890
around 36% of the white population. It's
also the native language of most Indian

00:10:29.890 --> 00:10:33.760
South Africans. they speak with an accent
distinct from white South African

00:10:33.760 --> 00:10:38.710
speakers of English. During the apartheid
era interaction with whites was limited,

00:10:38.710 --> 00:10:43.270
which allowed their own distinct variety
of English to develop in isolation, with

00:10:43.270 --> 00:10:47.000
some phonological influences from Indian
languages.

00:10:47.000 --> 00:10:52.090
I've mentioned the Khoisan languages a few times and you may be wondering why they aren't on the list of

00:10:52.090 --> 00:10:55.690
official languages.
Well, the Khoisan were the first

00:10:55.690 --> 00:10:59.620
indigenous people of much of South
Africa before the migration of Bantu

00:10:59.620 --> 00:11:03.190
peoples and later Europeans.
through conflict with Bantu and

00:11:03.190 --> 00:11:07.810
Europeans, Khoisan were driven into a
much smaller area and now most Khoisan

00:11:07.810 --> 00:11:12.370
live in the Kalahari Desert. Some Khoisan
intermarried with Europeans and became

00:11:12.370 --> 00:11:16.530
part of the colored population, while
others died from conflict and disease.

00:11:16.530 --> 00:11:21.220
The end result of all this is that only
a very small population of Khoisan

00:11:21.220 --> 00:11:24.910
remain in South Africa and their
Khoisan languages are therefore not

00:11:24.910 --> 00:11:28.000
widely spoken and are not official
languages.

00:11:28.100 --> 00:11:33.700
Khoisan languages do not forma single language family and are not accepted as being related to each other

00:11:33.700 --> 00:11:38.980
but all of them feature extensive use of
click consonants. As I stated before the

00:11:38.980 --> 00:11:43.000
click sounds used in Bantu languages
come from contact with Khoisan languages.

00:11:43.000 --> 00:11:48.190
The most widely spoken Khoisan language
in South Africa is Khoekhoe, or Nama,

00:11:48.190 --> 00:11:53.620
with around 50,000 speakers. This
language has an additional 250,000

00:11:53.620 --> 00:11:58.060
speakers in neighbouring Namibia. There
are other Khoisan languages in South

00:11:58.060 --> 00:12:04.000
Africa that are severely endangered like
Griqua, with only a few dozen speakers remaining.

00:12:04.700 --> 00:12:06.500
Language in society.

00:12:06.500 --> 00:12:12.370
In a society with such linguistic diversity, it's inevitable that the majority of the population will be bilingual or

00:12:12.370 --> 00:12:16.660
multilingual. Most white and coloured
South Africans are bilingual in English

00:12:16.660 --> 00:12:20.880
and Afrikaans, and a smaller number speak
one of the African languages as well.

00:12:20.880 --> 00:12:26.110
Indian South Africans speak English and
usually some Afrikaans and some speak an

00:12:26.110 --> 00:12:31.960
Indian language as well, typically older
people. Many black South Africans speak 3,

00:12:31.960 --> 00:12:36.190
4, 5 or even more languages. It's common
for them to speak their native African

00:12:36.190 --> 00:12:40.690
language, English, and Afrikaans and one
or more other African languages. They're

00:12:40.690 --> 00:12:44.170
often able to speak the other languages
of the same subgroup as their native

00:12:44.170 --> 00:12:48.310
language because they're closely related
and in many cases mutually intelligible.

00:12:48.310 --> 00:12:52.210
You may be wondering: "If the languages
within each subgroup are mutually

00:12:52.210 --> 00:12:54.970
intelligible, why are they considered
separate languages?"

00:12:54.970 --> 00:13:00.009
Well, what is a language versus what is a
dialect is a murky issue as always. But I

00:13:00.009 --> 00:13:04.179
think it's partly because they each have
their own standardized variety, and it's

00:13:04.179 --> 00:13:08.500
partly because each one is associated
with a distinct ethnic group.

00:13:08.600 --> 00:13:12.300
These days business and politics are mostly conducted in English,

00:13:12.300 --> 00:13:16.480
and English is the most common language of broadcast and media. For a long time,,

00:13:16.480 --> 00:13:20.949
English and Afrikaans had equal status
as dual lingua francas of South Africa

00:13:20.949 --> 00:13:25.449
but Afrikaans has been declining in that
regard. That's partly due to the stigma

00:13:25.449 --> 00:13:29.799
attached to Afrikaans as it's seen as
the language of apartheid. In addition to

00:13:29.799 --> 00:13:34.059
that, education in South Africa tends to
lean towards English. In typical South

00:13:34.059 --> 00:13:37.600
African schools, the first three grades
are taught in the students mother tongue

00:13:37.600 --> 00:13:43.209
but after that, school work is conducted
in either Afrikaans or English. Schools

00:13:43.209 --> 00:13:46.869
can teach in the native language longer,
but since secondary school matriculation

00:13:46.869 --> 00:13:51.579
exams are only conducted in English and
Afrikaans, there's a strong motivation to

00:13:51.579 --> 00:13:55.929
switch to one of those languages early
on. But rather than Afrikaans,

00:13:55.929 --> 00:14:00.669
most choose English due to its utility
and prestige. And in many cases, families

00:14:00.669 --> 00:14:04.029
will forego students being taught in
their first language and go straight

00:14:04.029 --> 00:14:08.350
into English to jumpstart their
proficiency. But whether this is a help

00:14:08.350 --> 00:14:11.100
or a hindrance is a source of much
debate.

00:14:11.100 --> 00:14:16.779
The emphasis on English and Afrikaans after the first three or four years of initial schooling is a lasting

00:14:16.779 --> 00:14:20.980
influence of the colonial era. Today
there are 11 official languages, and in

00:14:20.980 --> 00:14:25.419
theory and in law they are equal, but in
practice English and Afrikaans are still

00:14:25.419 --> 00:14:29.049
the most influential languages at the
national level. And this is still the

00:14:29.049 --> 00:14:33.200
case for now despite the fact that
Afrikaans has lost some of its prestige.

00:14:33.200 --> 00:14:36.999
The question of the day for South
Africans: Which languages do you speak

00:14:36.999 --> 00:14:41.829
and in what situations do you use them?
And for other people: How many official

00:14:41.829 --> 00:14:45.339
languages are there in your country? Do
you think that system works well for

00:14:45.339 --> 00:14:49.419
your country? Be sure to follow Langfocus on Facebook, Twitter, and Instagram.

00:14:49.419 --> 00:14:53.860
And once again thank you to all of my
wonderful Patreon supporters, especially

00:14:53.860 --> 00:14:58.239
these ones on the screen who are my top
tier Patreon supporters. Many thanks to

00:14:58.239 --> 00:15:01.500
them as always. And to everyone thank you
for watching and

00:15:01.500 --> 00:15:03.000
have a nice day.

00:15:18.670 --> 00:15:20.730
you

